IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc22-at-large.l4 -- sections 597 to 600 of the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46 (Part XX: proceedings when a person indicted is at large; change of venue). Part agent ccc22. Layer 1: the law, objectively.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30); it is not the official text, and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.
-- How a result reads: JUST TRUE (the text's own words bring the case within the rule); JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words); NOTHING (not covered). A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Tags in the comments: [M-a] a failed "if"/"where" or a flat negative counted as the text's no; [M-b] a prohibition or grant limited by its own wording counted as NOT the text's no (NOTHING);
-- [F:...] a fork line for NOTES.md (may-if, may-where, entitled, duty, unless). Section 2 terms and other parts' answers are INPUTS named for their section.

§ `Section 597 -- Bench warrant`

-- src:12712 | 597 (1) Where an indictment has been preferred against a person who is at large, and that person does not appear or remain in attendance for his trial, the court before which the accused should have appeared or remained in attendance may issue a warrant in Form 7 for his arrest.
@ref s 597(1) (src:12712-12712)
GIVEN `an indictment has been preferred against a person who is at large` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person does not appear or remain in attendance for their trial` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is the court before which the accused should have appeared or remained in attendance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 597(1) the court may issue a warrant in Form 7 for the arrest of the accused` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`an indictment has been preferred against a person who is at large` AND `the person does not appear or remain in attendance for their trial`) THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an indictment has been preferred against a person who is at large, and that person does not appear or remain in attendance for his trial" [F:may-where] [M-a]
        IF NOT `the court is the court before which the accused should have appeared or remained in attendance` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the court before which the accused should have appeared or remained in attendance may issue a warrant"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:12714 | (2) A warrant issued under subsection (1) may be executed anywhere in Canada.
@ref s 597(2) (src:12714-12714)
GIVEN `a warrant is issued under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 597(2) the warrant may be executed anywhere in Canada` MEANS
    IF `a warrant is issued under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A warrant issued under subsection (1)"

-- src:12716 | (3) If an accused is arrested under a warrant issued under subsection (1), a judge of the court that issued the warrant may make a release order referred to in section 515.
@ref s 597(3) (src:12716-12716)
GIVEN `the accused is arrested under a warrant issued under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a judge of the court that issued the warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 597(3) a judge of the court that issued the warrant may make a release order referred to in section 515` MEANS
    IF `the accused is arrested under a warrant issued under subsection (1)` AND `the person is a judge of the court that issued the warrant`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "If an accused is arrested under a warrant issued under subsection (1), a judge of the court that issued the warrant may make a release order" [F:may-if]

-- src:12718 | (4) A judge who issues a warrant may specify in the warrant the period before which the warrant shall not be executed, to allow the accused to appear voluntarily before a judge having jurisdiction in the territorial division in which the warrant was issued.
@ref s 597(4) (src:12718-12718)
GIVEN `the judge issues the warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
